<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="TcpServer.UI.app" />

  <!-- Added 2026-09-14: application manifest.
       Purpose: run with the current user's privileges, and declare support for Windows 7 through 11.
       Note: this file intentionally does NOT declare high-DPI awareness.
             See the reverting notes at the end of this file. -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <!-- Run with the current user's privileges; no elevation is requested -->
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 / Windows 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
      <!-- Windows 8.1 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{1f676c76-80e1-4239-95bb-83d0f6d0da78}" />
      <!-- Windows 8 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{4a2f28e3-53b9-4441-ba9c-d69d4a4a6e38}" />
      <!-- Windows 7 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{35138b9a-5d96-4fbd-8e2d-a2440225f93a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <!-- ============================================================================
       2026-09-14 REVERTED: high-DPI awareness declaration (dpiAware / dpiAwareness)

       [Why it was reverted]
       The development machine runs at 150% scaling (registry AppliedDPI = 0x90 = 144).
       Declaring DPI awareness produced two side effects:
         1) The window is rendered at 1:1 physical pixels and is no longer stretched 1.5x
            by the system bitmap scaler, so the whole UI looks smaller;
         2) WinForms AutoScaleMode=Font derives its scale factor from Font.Height, which
            .NET always computes at 96 DPI regardless of the real DPI, so the factor stays
            1.0 and control sizes are never enlarged, while GDI+ still renders text at
            144 DPI. Text then overflows buttons and labels ("Generate Ports" was clipped)
            and several controls on the top toolbar became overlapped.
       Result: smaller UI plus overlapping controls, i.e. a regression, so it was rolled back.

       [How it was rolled back]
       Comment out this node, and also comment out the
       <System.Windows.Forms.ApplicationConfigurationSection> node in App.config.
       Nothing else is affected. Once the default bitmap stretching is restored,
       the ratio between window size and font size returns to normal.

       [If it is ever enabled again (the correct way)]
       All of the following must be changed together; missing any one of them is not enough:
         1) Restore this node;
         2) Restore <System.Windows.Forms.ApplicationConfigurationSection> in App.config;
         3) Change AutoScaleMode from Font to Dpi and AutoScaleDimensions to (96F, 96F)
            on all three forms, otherwise scaling is still missed because Font.Height
            does not follow the DPI;
         4) After the change, verify on a real machine that the top toolbar
            (11 controls occupying 1028 px in total) is not clipped.

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2, PerMonitor</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
       ============================================================================ -->
</assembly>
